---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import DocsHandoff from '../components/DocsHandoff.astro';
import { askQuestionHref, describeServiceHref } from '../data/contact';

const pageTitle = 'FAQ | Registry Stack';
const pageDescription =
  'Answers for government teams about Registry Stack. Read about service fit, control of records, starting configurations, implementation, costs and support.';

// Grouped so a reader can scan to their own concern. Each answer stays short and
// links to the page that carries the depth, so the FAQ does not become a second
// copy of the site.
const groups = [
  {
    "heading": "Fit for your service",
    "items": [
      {
        "q": "What does Registry Stack help us do?",
        "a": "Institutions use Registry Stack to maintain registers and to review requests that need a staff decision. It also lets other services check information. For example, a licensing authority can use it to keep licence records and assign renewal requests to officers. Another service can then check the recorded status of a licence.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Explore the solution areas",
          "href": "/solutions/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "Which products do we need?",
        "a": "Choose by the work your service needs. This can be maintaining records, reviewing requests, generating a document, sending a notice or checking information. You can use one product. Or you can connect several products with your existing systems.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Compare the products",
          "href": "/products/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "Is Registry Stack for national, regional or local government?",
        "a": "It can support all three. A national regulator, a regional administration or a local authority can use it. Each one can adapt the records, access rules and review steps to its responsibilities. Describe the service you need, for example licensing, business registration or a farmer register.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Explore the solution areas",
          "href": "/solutions/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "Who is behind Registry Stack?",
        "a": "We are Aubex Consulting LLC. We build and support Registry Stack. We also offer paid implementation and technical support. We are based in San Francisco, United States.",
        "link": {
          "label": "See our company details",
          "href": "/imprint/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "Can we keep our existing register?",
        "a": "Yes, if your existing system can provide the information needed. Evidence Gateway is the Registry Stack product for information checks. For example, it can let another service check a supplier’s registration status while your register stays in its current system. The implementation work checks how to connect the systems. You decide what another service may receive. The rules are configured to match.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Read about Evidence Gateway",
          "href": "/products/evidence-gateway/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "What do we need to add to make a working service?",
        "a": "The core products handle records, staff reviews and information checks. A working service also needs user screens, sign-in and connections to other systems. It also needs arrangements for hosting and support. Your own team or an implementation partner does this work. Pilot register implementation can include the parts you agree with us.",
        "link": {
          "label": "See the implementation options",
          "href": "/pricing/"
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "Your records and implementation",
    "items": [
      {
        "q": "Who controls our records?",
        "a": "You set the access rules. They say who may view, add or change records. They also say what other services may receive. Your institution or a provider it appoints runs the software. Or we run it for you. Your institution’s team controls the systems the deployment connects to.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Read about security and responsibilities",
          "href": "/security/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "What will our team need to do?",
        "a": "You define the service rules. You approve who may access or receive information. You name the people responsible for the service and its records. You also agree how to check that the service meets your requirements. The proposal assigns the remaining delivery and handover work to your own team, to us or to another provider.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Read about implementation and support",
          "href": "/pricing/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "Can we start from a spreadsheet or an existing database?",
        "a": "Yes, with data preparation. Your own team can prepare existing data for a new register or for specific information checks. The implementation must define how fields match and how errors are checked. It must also define how the data is kept up to date. This work is agreed as part of the implementation.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Read how a service is adapted",
          "href": "/customization/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "Can one connection support more than one information check?",
        "a": "Yes, when the checks use the same source system and the same access arrangements. For example, a connection to a company register could support checks of both registration status and registered address. You still define what each check may return and who may request it. Other sources may need separate connections.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Read about information checks",
          "href": "/solutions/check-a-fact/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "How do we know whether information is up to date?",
        "a": "The time an answer was checked is different from the time the source record was updated. The implementation needs rules for how recent the information must be, especially when it uses a copy of a register. A digital signature lets the receiving service check who issued the answer and whether it was altered. It does not prove that the source information is current or correct.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Read about information checks and dates",
          "href": "/solutions/check-a-fact/#faq"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "Can we issue verifiable credentials?",
        "a": "Yes. For example, a professional council can issue a digitally signed statement of licence status. The professional keeps it in a digital wallet and presents it to an employer. The implementation chooses the wallet. It tests the full journey before launch, including the employer’s receiving service. A credential shows the licence status on the day it was issued. It stays valid until the expiry date you set.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Read about verifiable credentials",
          "href": "/solutions/verifiable-credentials/"
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "Starting configurations",
    "items": [
      {
        "q": "What is a starting configuration?",
        "a": "A starting configuration is also called a starter. It defines the records, the permitted changes and the access roles for one kind of register. Your own team can adapt it to your rules. It is a starting point. The screens, system connections and operating arrangements still need to be supplied.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Browse starting configurations",
          "href": "https://marketplace.registrystack.org/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "How do we access a starting configuration?",
        "a": "Access is free. Use the access request on the configuration’s page in the marketplace. If you are unsure which one fits, tell us what your institution needs. We review the request and reply.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Find a starting configuration",
          "href": "https://marketplace.registrystack.org/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "Do we need to choose a configuration before we contact you?",
        "a": "No. Describe the service, who will use it and what it needs to do. We can help you find a suitable starting configuration. We can also discuss the changes it needs.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Describe your service",
          "href": describeServiceHref('a service that needs a starting configuration')
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "Costs, evaluation and support",
    "items": [
      {
        "q": "Is there a software licence fee?",
        "a": "Base Registry Engine, Evidence Gateway and Registry Casework are open source under the Apache-2.0 licence. There is no software licence fee for them. Implementation and technical support are paid services. Separate products such as Registry Studio have their own terms.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Read the licensing summary",
          "href": "/terms/#terms-license-title"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "Do we have to buy support to use the software?",
        "a": "No. You can use the core software with your own team or a provider you choose. You are then responsible for arranging hosting and maintenance. The licence and attribution requirements still apply.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Use Registry Stack with your own team",
          "href": "/pricing/#self-service"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "How mature is the software?",
        "a": "Registry Stack is released in versions. Each release has published release notes and documentation. Your institution or a provider it appoints runs it. Or we run it for you. Before work starts, you agree with us in writing who is responsible for hosting, support and upgrades.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Read the known limitations",
          "href": "https://docs.registrystack.org/explanation/known-limitations/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "How can our technical team evaluate it?",
        "a": "Start with the introductory tutorials. They cover creating a small register, setting permissions and trying an information check. Your technical team can then review the documentation and the source code against your requirements. The documentation also has the setup and configuration instructions.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Choose an introductory tutorial",
          "href": "/#developers"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "What implementation and support services are available?",
        "a": "Pilot register implementation means we implement one register and its service with your own team. We configure it to your rules and test it with your staff. The proposal states what is included. It also states whether your pilot register runs with test data or live records. Annual technical support is available for teams that implement and operate Registry Stack themselves. We agree the work, the responsibilities and the fee with you in writing before work starts.",
        "link": {
          "label": "See implementation and support pricing",
          "href": "/pricing/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "Can AI help us design or adapt a service?",
        "a": "Yes. AI can help your own team turn research and service requirements into a brief. It can use your existing forms when useful. When we implement your pilot register, we use AI to draft configuration and test cases for its records, forms and review steps. You review and approve the rules. The configured service does not need an AI model to run. Registry Studio is an authoring workspace in Preview. It includes document-assisted drafting. AI is there to help people. It is not an autonomous product.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Read where AI fits in public services",
          "href": "/ai/"
        }
      },
      {
        "q": "Can someone use an AI assistant to make a request?",
        "a": "Yes, in a Preview integration for a configured Base Registry Engine service. An approved assistant helps a signed-in person prepare a change request and check its status. The person reviews and submits the request on your institution’s site. The assistant cannot submit a request, approve it or change a record. You choose which assistants and actions to allow.",
        "link": {
          "label": "Read about citizen access through an assistant",
          "href": "/ai/citizen-access/"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
];

// FAQPage structured data, built from every group's question and answer so the
// markup stays in step with the visible FAQ.
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: groups.flatMap((group) =>
    group.items.map((item) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: item.q,
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: item.a,
      },
    })),
  ),
};
---

<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <SiteHeader current="faq" />

  <main id="main-content" tabindex="-1">
    <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="faq-hero-title">
      <div class="hero-inner">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p>
          <h1 id="faq-hero-title">Frequently asked questions.</h1>
          <p class="hero-lead">Find out how Registry Stack can fit your service. See who controls your records. Learn what implementation and support involve.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {groups.map((group, index) => (
      <section class={index % 2 === 1 ? 'section section-muted' : 'section'} aria-labelledby={`faq-group-${index}`}>
        <div class="section-inner">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <h2 id={`faq-group-${index}`}>{group.heading}</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-list">
            {group.items.map((item) => (
              <details class="faq-item">
                <summary>{item.q}</summary>
                <div class="faq-answer">
                  <p>{item.a}</p>
                  <a class="inline-cta" href={item.link.href}>{item.link.label} &rarr;</a>
                </div>
              </details>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}
  </main>

  <DocsHandoff
    heading="Do you still have a question?"
    body="Tell us about the service you need and what you would like to know."
    primaryLabel="Ask a question"
    primaryHref={askQuestionHref()}
    emphasis="docs"
  />
  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .faq-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: clamp(20px, 3vw, 28px);
    max-width: 820px;
  }

  .faq-item {
    background: var(--registry-white);
    border: 1px solid var(--registry-rule);
  }

  .faq-item summary {
    align-items: center;
    color: var(--registry-ink);
    cursor: pointer;
    display: flex;
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    font-weight: 700;
    gap: 16px;
    justify-content: space-between;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 18px 20px;
  }

  .faq-item summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  /* A register tick that opens into a cross, so the control reads without
     relying on a dash glyph. */
  .faq-item summary::after {
    color: var(--registry-blue);
    content: '+';
    font-family: var(--registry-mono);
    font-size: var(--type-subhead);
    line-height: 1;
    transition: transform 0.15s ease;
  }

  .faq-item[open] summary::after {
    transform: rotate(45deg);
  }

  .faq-item summary:hover {
    color: var(--registry-blue);
  }

  .faq-answer {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--registry-rule);
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 16px 20px 20px;
  }

  .faq-answer p {
    color: var(--registry-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .faq-item summary::after {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
